    <!-- Shared language -->
    <div class="docs-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
      <h2 id="shared-language" class="docs-section-title">
        Shared language: one name for one thing
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
        "
      >
        The most common fault in the design systems the course's authors audit
        is naming inconsistency: <code>size="large"</code> on one component and
        <code>size="lg"</code> on the next, token names built by two different
        rules, layers called <code>Frame</code>, documentation that disagrees
        with design and code. An agent has no way to know that two spellings
        mean one thing. It treats each as a separate fact, so it guesses, and
        the guess goes into code. The course's phrase for the fix is that
        consistent language is the contract that lets AI reason about your
        design system.
      </p>
      <h3
        style="
          font-size: var(--ui-font-size-lg);
          font-weight: 600;
          margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
        "
      >
        Do it in this order
      </h3>
      <ol
        style="
          margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
          padding-left: 1.25rem;
          line-height: 1.6;
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
        "
      >
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">Report.</strong> Have an
          agent list every name in use, per category, with where each appears.
          Read-only, nothing is renamed yet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">Decide.</strong> You pick
          one word per concept and write the vocabulary down. The agent can
          propose; it cannot know which spelling your production code already
          depends on.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">Rename once.</strong>
          Apply the vocabulary across Figma and code in one pass, then review
          each asset. Anything already in production keeps the old name as a
          deprecated alias for a stated window. The course's demo renames
          <code>inverted</code> to <code>knockout</code> this way; the full
          explanation is in
          <a href="/tokens#naming" class="docs-link-primary">Tokens, naming</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">Keep it.</strong> Turn the
          vocabulary into a check that fails the build. A report that nobody
          reruns decays.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        "
      >
        Four categories are worth a column each in the report: variant property
        names and values (<code>size</code>, <code>lg</code>), token names,
        layer names, and the words in descriptions and docs. This prompt works
        on your own file. It is read-only: it uses figma-console-mcp reads and
        never writes to Figma.
      </p>
      <Code
        code={`Consistency report, read-only. Do not rename or write anything.

1. Figma: using figma-console-mcp read tools (figma_get_variables,
   figma_analyze_component_set, figma_search_components, and a read-only
   figma_execute walk where no tool exists), list across ALL masters:
   - every variant property name, with its values and the masters using it
   - every variable name, grouped by prefix, noting where two different
     naming rules are in use
   - every layer name that is a default (Frame, Group, Rectangle) and
     layer names that differ between variants of one master
2. Code: from the component types or manifest, list every prop name and
   every string-union value, with the component that declares it.
3. Report, per category, every concept that has more than one spelling
   (large / lg, outline / outlined, above / top), the count of uses of each
   spelling, and the components affected.
4. Mark which spelling you would pick and why. Do not apply anything.
   List which names look like they are used by shipped code.`}
        lang="text"
        title="Consistency report prompt"
      />
      <MaintainerNotes topic="Atelier gates and consistency scan (2026-10-09)">
        <h3
          style="
            font-size: var(--ui-font-size-lg);
            font-weight: 600;
            margin: 1.5rem 0 0.6rem;
          "
        >
          What Atelier enforces, and what it does not
        </h3>
        <p
          style="
            color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
            line-height: 1.6;
            margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
          "
        >
          Atelier's gates check that two places agree, not that the vocabulary
          is consistent across components. Each of these joins Figma to code, or
          the spec to the adapters, for one component at a time:
        </p>
        <table class="docs-props-table" style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Gate</th>
              <th>Checks</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>check:contracts</code></td>
              <td>
                A master's axis names and values against the component's own
                manifest and stories (<code>[AXIS]</code>). A deliberate
                difference is written down as an <code>axisMap</code> entry with
                a reason.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>check:figma</code></td>
              <td>
                A master's variant axis against the spec field or union it names
                (<code>AXIS-NAME</code>, <code>AXIS-NOT-A-PROP</code>), and that
                named layers resolve to a CSS rule (ADR-0063: the layer name is
                the selector). A layer still called <code>Frame</code> is
                counted, not judged.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>check:figma-token-names</code></td>
              <td>
                Every <code>color/*</code>, <code>spacing/*</code> and
                <code>radius/*</code> variable in the Figma snapshot resolves to
                a <code>--ui-*</code> declaration in <code>tokens.css</code>.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>check:variants</code>, <code>check:props</code></td>
              <td>
                Each spec union member has a CSS class in every framework, and
                each framework declares every spec prop.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p
          style="
            color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
            line-height: 1.6;
            margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
          "
        >
          <code>axisMap</code> is where Atelier records a name that differs on
          purpose. This is the entry on <code>AtlTh</code>:
        </p>
        <Code
          code={`axisMap: [
  {
    figmaAxis: 'sortDirection',
    codeProp: 'sortDirection',
    values: { none: null, asc: 'asc', desc: 'desc' },
    reason: "Same prop name, but the axis value 'none' stands for the code union's null ..."
  },
],`}
          lang="ts"
          title="libs/spec/src/contracts/th.contract.ts (shortened)"
        />
        <p
          style="
            color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
            line-height: 1.6;
            margin: 1.25rem 0;
          "
        >
          None of the scripts above compares one component's vocabulary with
          another's. A search of <code>tools/scripts</code> on 2026-10-09 found
          no gate that does; the only hit for the word <em>vocabulary</em> in
          <code>tools/scripts</code> is a comment in <code>check-parity.js</code
          >. So the consistency itself is a convention, kept by whoever adds the
          next component.
        </p>
        <Callout
          tone="note"
          title="What a consistency pass found in Atelier's spec (2026-10-09)"
        >
          <p
            style="
              margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
              line-height: 1.6;
              color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
            "
          >
            A read-only scan of the string unions in
            <code>libs/spec/src/index.ts</code>, plus the axes in
            <code>tools/figma/snapshot.json</code>. Nothing was changed.
          </p>
          <ul
            style="
              margin: 0;
              padding-left: 1.25rem;
              line-height: 1.6;
              color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
            "
          >
            <li>
              <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
                >Size is consistent.</strong
              >
              The <code>Atl*Size</code> unions use only <code>xs</code>,
              <code>sm</code>, <code>md</code>, <code>lg</code>, <code>xl</code>
              and <code>full</code>. None spells out <code>large</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
                >Position is not.</strong
              >
              <code>AtlTooltipPosition</code> is
              <code>above | below | left | right</code>;
              <code>AtlDrawerPosition</code> is
              <code>left | right | top | bottom</code>. Same prop name, two
              words for the vertical sides.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
                >Outline has two spellings.</strong
              >
              <code>AtlButtonVariant</code> has <code>outline</code>;
              <code>AtlCardVariant</code> has <code>outlined</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
                >Failure is named two ways.</strong
              >
              <code>danger</code> is the variant in button, badge, alert, toast
              and progress; the Figma <code>state</code> axis uses both
              <code>error</code> and <code>invalid</code>, and
              <code>AtlIconName</code> has both <code>danger</code> and
              <code>error</code>. Whether each pair is one concept or two was
              not checked.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </Callout>
      </MaintainerNotes>
    </div>

    <!-- Code-only facts -->
    <div class="docs-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
      <h2 id="code-only" class="docs-section-title">
        Facts the design cannot show: code-only props
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
        "
      >
        Some facts about a component never appear in the design: the native
        <code>type</code> of a button, an <code>aria-label</code>, an image's
        <code>alt</code> text, how it should behave. An agent that only sees the
        design misses them. The "code-only props" technique, credited to Nathan
        Curtis, puts them in a hidden layer inside each component, so an agent
        reading the node finds them there.
      </p>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
        "
      >
        Atelier keeps the same facts in the master's description instead, one
        line each. This is the line on <code>AtlButton</code>:
      </p>
      <Code
        code={
          '- Code-only `type`: native button type (button | submit | reset) — form semantics the element always carries, not a visual axis.'
        }
        lang="text"
        title="AtlButton master description"
      />
      <MaintainerNotes topic="how Atelier gates code-only lines, ADR-0161">
        <p
          style="
            color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
            line-height: 1.6;
            margin: 1.25rem 0;
          "
        >
          <code>check:contracts</code> joins this line to the component's
          contract in both directions. Every <code>codeOnly</code> entry in the
          contract needs a matching line (<code>[UNMIRRORED]</code>), and every
          <code>- Code-only</code> line must name a prop or event that a
          framework manifest has (<code>[STALE-MIRROR]</code>). Both are
          warnings.
        </p>
        <p
          style="
            color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
            line-height: 1.6;
            margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
          "
        >
          The two are not the same thing. The contract's <code>codeOnly</code>
          is narrower: props with no Figma axis, each with a reason. The
          course's layer holds any non-visual fact, including ones no contract
          lists.
        </p>
        <Callout tone="note" title="Why Atelier did not adopt the hidden layer">
          <p
            style="
              margin: 0;
              line-height: 1.6;
              color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
            "
          >
            The snapshot walks hidden layers on purpose, so a hidden frame trips
            several gates and each would need an exemption. A layer lives per
            variant, so <code>AtlButton</code> alone would carry 24 copies with
            nothing to keep them in sync. And nothing in the repo reads a
            layer's text, while tools already return the description. A team can
            still add the layer in its own file; just count those costs first.
            The decision is recorded as ADR-0161.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </MaintainerNotes>
    </div>

